00:00:05.790 --> 00:00:08.059 align:start position:0%
 
my name is Diane Wirth and I'm professor  of immunology and infectious disease at  the Harvard th Chan School of Public  Health I'm here today interviewing dr.  Kent Kester on his work in malaria ken  please introduce yourself I'm Ken Kester  I'm professor medicine uniformed  services University and head of  translational science and biomarkers at  sanofi pasteur can you tell me how you  first got involved to work on malaria  what were your first experiences it was  kind of an accidental thing I was an  infectious disease fellow in the early  1990s at Walter Reed Army Medical Center  and ended up working doing my research  rotation as an infectious disease fellow  with people who I knew in the Army who  ended up working on malaria one thing  led to another and research project  ended my fellowship ended I graduated  stayed on at Walter Reed and and ended  up doing malaria vaccine development and  more for probably the next 20 years in  the army  what do you think the biggest R&amp;D  challenges are that we face today and  then maybe we've paid faced over the  last decade from my perspective it's one  of the biggest ones is funding we  recognize that that developing effective  interventions whether that's drugs or  vaccines against malaria requires  sustained funding and piecemeal funding  small grants and awards just don't do it  and if you're going to develop something  that's going to have a global  population-based impact it really has to  be done at an industrial scale so  funding is number one number two is is  sustaining interest on the part of the  scientific community there's no shortage  of research scientists who like to work  on malaria they have their their  favorite enzyme their antigens and it's  all good stuff but the challenge is is  that as sort of the the behemoth of  scientific funding and areas of emphasis  move along from innovative area to newer  innovative areas sometimes interest gets  lost and so it's important to maintain  that interest to grow next generation  of researchers and to keep keep the  pressure on to get things done you've  been in the military and now you're in  industry and you've been exposed to the  world health organization and  public-private partnerships can you give  us a sense of the roles of these  organizations how they differ and how  they've changed over the years  you know I think one of the big impacts  of sort of the worldwide malaria  research malaria R&amp;D research  elimination eradication campaigns has  been this concept of public/private  partnerships it's clear that academic  groups well they have great ideas great  science a lot of enthusiastic motivated  young researchers don't necessarily have  the wherewithal to get products across  the finish line industry while can do  things at a big scale can get a lot of  things made and and deployed have the  regulatory throughput don't always  necessarily have that spark of  early-stage discovery interest so that  that's a disconnect government agencies  nonprofits I think they tend to fill in  the gaps and so it really is an  important concept this public-private  partnerships it's an it's an evolving  one I think where we are now as to where  we were say in the early 90s we're light  years ahead in thinking about the  development say of the RTSs vaccine  which you had familiarity with as you  were working in the military and and now  in your role at Sanofi looking at  vaccines more generally what made that  something to push forward as you say it  took a long time the RTSs vaccine was  decades in development and but now it is  an effective vaccine in humans I think  there was much work done with our CSS  and its predecessors for a number of  years that didn't really go anywhere a  lot of failed trials small studies 5 10  15 volunteers and it was really the  advent of combining RTSs the antigen  with novel adjuvant that had been  developed at that time by by SmithKline  Beecham now GlaxoSmithKline that really  showed for the first time that effective  actually could be achieved I think if  that hadn't happened the program  probably would have died that finding of  significant protection it really I don't  want to say it put the program on  steroids but it certainly propelled it  better and and made believers of people  who previously had thought that it  wasn't going to go anywhere as you think  to the future where do you see malaria  vaccine development going and where do  you see it kind of fitting into the  overall vaccine development the world  the vaccines has changed and and where  does malaria fit in this newer world  it's a great question you know if you  look at our TSS in many ways as you say  it's gone through really decades of work  to get where we are now it's kind of an  old-style vaccine that's not necessarily  a bad thing but if we were starting de  novo today we probably would do things a  lot differently  we have much different insights into  into malaria immunology protection  against disease in some ways analogous  to what's happened on a larger scale in  HIV so the whole you know fight against  HIV has just developed huge amounts of  knowledge about immunology and and I  think the RTSs work and and allied  efforts your other groups who want to  develop another vaccine or have  different antigenic idea building on the  work that the RTSs program has has has  done I think has really helped us a lot  we have a vaccine that that clearly  shows some degree of protection as you  say it has its advantages and it has its  limitations I think now we're at a point  where we can take a step back the field  and say okay how can we make this better  how can we leverage new technologies  whether it's genomics or other types of  omics things you know new ways of  interrogating the immune response and  really discerning correlates of  protection etc so I think it's really  been groundbreaking the only thing  that's been a bit discouraging of course  which goes back to my comment before  about funding it's taken a long time so  if you look in the industry you know if  you have an idea let's say for a  quote-unquote routine vaccine  whatever that means from from idea to  approval to deployment may take 15 to 20  years it may cost up to a billion  dollars for all the trials all the  production all that kind of stuff and if  you look at the amount of funding that's  gone to malaria vaccines well it's a lot  of money it's not a billion dollars yeah  it's it's a very interesting perspective  in thinking about vaccines in general I  mean there's this push now to get a Zika  vaccine and we hear that Zika vaccines  will be available really quickly so in  contrast to this long development period  what what makes that technologically  possible but also what makes that  possible and kind of been an advocacy  sense the big difference is of course is  malaria parasite so parasites have  evolved with us you know for millennia  and and so they know that they know how  to hide out they know how to how to live  with us in most cases there are multiple  antigenic variations of parasites so  that's part of it Zika it's one strain  it's a virus we know how to make viral  vaccines you know it's it's fairly  straightforward and I'm not saying that  that's necessarily a slam-dunk but it's  much easier than developing a vaccine  against the virus  there's already been a dengue vaccine  that that's been licensed a number of  countries around the world there's  yellow fever vaccine and there are other  vaccine candidates against other types  of flaviviruses that in experimental  settings actually work very well I think  that the concept of getting a Zika  vaccine technically made and and along  the approval pathway I think that's not  out of the question I think the bigger  challenge for Zika is how do you show  efficacy in a setting of perhaps an  unstable epidemic which we still don't  understand when eighty percent of the  people are actually asymptomatic so  there there's still some unknowns there  but I think from a technical standpoint  developing a Zika vaccine is is much  much easier so in some ways vaccine like  the yellow fever vaccine which was one  of the first successful vaccines led the  way in in some ways you can think about  RTSs maybe leading the way for malaria  vaccines in the same way because the  parasite is much more complex so I think  that  very interesting analogy to how to think  about where we are in malaria vaccine  development absolutely you show some  initial protection you show initial  value and if there's a need hopefully  the funders the advocacy groups and the  scientists who have the good ideas will  be able to run with it and I think  that's what we're seeing in terms of  eradication which is the kind of focus  of what we're talking about in general  do you think the vaccine is needs to be  there in order for eradication to be  successful you know if you if I think  about it you know eradication the  diseases that are eradicated or almost  eradicated in the world primarily are  through the use of a vaccine so do you  think it's possible to eradicate malaria  without a vaccine and I'd be interested  in your thoughts on that it's an  interesting question I you know as a  vaccine person and as a malaria vaccine  person of course I have to say yes yes  that it's important but but from a when  one takes a step back when we think  about controlling eradicating  eliminating malaria a lot of the  approaches are low-tech right get rid of  mosquitoes treat diagnose and treat  people get rid of standing water these  sorts of things  however again these are parasites  they're very wily parasites they're very  persistent if one is trying to develop  sort of a comprehensive approach to  eliminate malaria I think there's a role  for a vaccine just as much as there's a  role for mosquito control and bed nets  and and more what sort of advice would  you give someone who's kind of early in  their career or even pre career on on  what how to prepare themselves for a  leadership role as you've assumed in  your field I think part of it is is  don't just don't just limit oneself to  to excellence at the bench that's really  important you have to be scientifically  credible however if you really want to  make a difference you've got to  understand the issues you have to  understand how does a vaccine to fit  into a larger strategy you know how does  that fit into low-tech approaches and  public policy and whu-oh recommendations  etc etc from a realistic standpoint they  really need to be engaged just beyond  the bench you know understand the issues  make context and and really be involved  you know and it's not it's not  necessarily political advocacy it's it's  its scientific connectivity and under  and being engaged and being visible  that's a great way to think about it  having them reach out to others that  have complementary skills right you and  I have both dealt with many people in  our careers  who've shown an interest and we've  helped point them in those directions  and I think we can count a number of  those people who are now developing  leadership roles of their own right  which is a really good thing yeah well  thank you very much Kent this has been  fantastic great thanks

